[{:db/id -1, :adr/id "adr-2605231401-karute-consent-capability-iryo-bridge", :adr/title "ADR-2605231401: karute consent capability + iryo billing bridge", :adr/status "proposed", :adr/doc_type "adr", :adr/source-format "edn", :adr/body "\n# ADR-2605231401: karute consent capability + iryo billing bridge\n\n**Status**: proposed\n**Date**: 2026-05-23\n**Deciders**: Jun Kawasaki\n\n# Context\n\nADR-2605231100 (karute EMR Phase 1) deferred insurance billing to a follow-up consent-capability ADR. The 3-axis split rule (ADR-2605172400) puts clinical PHI custody on the etzhayyim substrate but JP insurance claim submission (社保 / 国保 → DPC/DRG) on the vendor `iryo.etzhayyim.com` actor because:\n\n1. **Settlement** is fiat (社保支払基金 / 国保連 → bank wire). The etzhayyim substrate restricts payments to USDC/ERC-4337 (ADR-2605172100). Self-pay clinics work day one; insurance-clinics need vendor.\n2. **Custody** for billed claims is regulatory (社保レセプト保管義務). Vendor side holds the claim record; etzhayyim holds the source clinical record. Single-system custody is constitutionally precluded.\n3. **Liability** for claim accuracy attaches to the billing entity (vendor commercial liability). The clinical record's accuracy liability attaches to the treating clinician (etzhayyim DID).\n\nThe forces are:\n\n- **Patient consent must be explicit, scope-limited, revocable.** \"I clicked Login\" is not consent for billing data forwarding. Each purpose (billing / second-opinion / research) requires its own grant.\n- **etzhayyim cannot trust vendor's storage.** If vendor leaks, the patient must be able to show they only granted a narrow scope, and to revoke prospectively.\n- **The grant must work with the encrypted-record envelope.** Ciphertext is on MST; only read-cap holders see plaintext. The capability MUST translate into a key-wrap-equivalent that vendor can present at the karute substrate to obtain a read-cap for the in-scope records.\n- **Cross-actor invocation needs a constitutional path.** The etzhayyim charter (ADR-2605192100 + 2605192115) prohibits direct vendor-substrate writes from etzhayyim apps; consent capability is the only ADR-approved bridge.\n\n# Decision\n\n## The consent capability primitive\n\nA consent capability is an Ed25519-signed delegation record stored at `com.etzhayyim.consent.capability` in the granter's PDS. Shape:\n\n```\n{\n  version: 1,\n  granterDid:    \"did:plc:patient-self-or-guardian\",\n  granteeDid:    \"did:web:iryo.etzhayyim.com\" | \"did:web:dr-someone.etzhayyim.com\" | ...,\n  purpose:       \"insurance-billing\" | \"second-opinion\" | \"data-portability\"\n                | \"research-deidentified\" | \"emergency-disclosure\" | \"legal-disclosure\",\n  scope:         [\"com.etzhayyim.karute.encounter\", \"com.etzhayyim.karute.serviceRequest\", ...],\n  resourceUris:  [optional AT URI allowlist — narrower than scope],\n  issuedAt:      \"ISO-8601\",\n  expiresAt:     \"ISO-8601\",\n  constraints: {\n    maxQueriesPerDay?:           int,\n    redactionLevel?:             \"raw\" | \"de-identified\" | \"aggregate-only\",\n    downstreamRedistribution?:   bool (default false),\n    auditWebhookDid?:            DID receiving each invocation event\n  },\n  delegationPath?: [DID...],     // guardian / institution proxy chain\n  revokedAt?:    \"ISO-8601\",\n  revokedBy?:    DID,\n  revocationReason?: string,\n  signature:     { alg: \"ed25519\", value: base64, keyId: DID-verification-method }\n}\n```\n\nThe signature covers the canonicalized payload (everything but `signature` itself, sorted-keys CBOR per AT Protocol convention). The record itself is **NOT encrypted** — capability metadata is not PHI, and visibility supports public verifiability of the grant. The records the capability points to remain encrypted.\n\n## Three usage patterns\n\n### Pattern 1 — Internal etzhayyim (clinician ↔ clinician, peer review)\n\nA patient grants `did:web:dr-cardiology.etzhayyim.com` a `second-opinion` capability scoped to `com.etzhayyim.karute.encounter` + `com.etzhayyim.karute.condition` + `com.etzhayyim.karute.observation` for 30 days.\n\nThe grantee actor presents the capability to the karute substrate alongside its own DID, the substrate verifies signature + scope + expiry, and emits one `com.etzhayyim.encrypted.keyWrap` record per in-scope encrypted-record CID encrypted to the grantee's Signal session. The grantee's @etzhayyim/sdk then performs normal `encryptedRead`.\n\n### Pattern 2 — etzhayyim ↔ vendor bridge (insurance billing)\n\nA patient grants `did:web:iryo.etzhayyim.com` an `insurance-billing` capability scoped to the encounter + service-request + medication-request URIs for a specific encounter. The karute pipeline calls iryo's `ingestKaruteEncounterForBilling` XRPC, passing the capability URI. iryo:\n\n1. Resolves the capability record (public, signed)\n2. Verifies signature against the patient's DID document\n3. Verifies `purpose === \"insurance-billing\"` and `granteeDid === did:web:iryo.etzhayyim.com`\n4. Verifies the requested scope is within the capability scope and timestamp is within validity\n5. Issues a key-wrap request to the karute substrate (which the substrate honors because the capability is valid)\n6. Decrypts each in-scope record via `@etzhayyim/sdk.encryptedRead`\n7. Materializes the DPC/DRG claim into its own Kotoba/Datomic (subject to standard JP保険 retention rules)\n8. Forwards to 社保支払基金 / 国保連 via 既存 vendor channels\n9. Returns claim reference + initial status\n\n**Vendor commercial liability for claim accuracy is unchanged**; the consent capability transfers data access, not legal responsibility.\n\n### Pattern 3 — Patient-initiated data portability\n\nA patient exporting their chart to a non-etzhayyim EHR grants `did:web:external-ehr.example` a `data-portability` capability scoped to all karute inner-types, time-limited (e.g. 7 days). The external EHR uses the same key-wrap flow.\n\nIf the external EHR is not on the etzhayyim substrate, the patient performs the decrypt on their own device (via `karute.exportFhirBundle` from the patient portal) and uploads the FHIR Bundle to the external system. The consent capability is then primarily an audit artifact rather than a runtime access token.\n\n## Revocation semantics\n\nRevocation is observable but not retroactive — the record's `revokedAt` is set, but ciphertext already fetched by the grantee cannot be recalled. This is acknowledged in ADR-2605181100 §5 \"metadata leakage that this design does not solve.\"\n\nMitigations:\n- The grantee MUST cooperate by treating revoked capabilities as immediately void per the grantee's clinician/vendor agreement.\n- Auditor webhooks (`constraints.auditWebhookDid`) receive both invocation and revocation events, providing a public timeline a regulator can subpoena.\n- For high-sensitivity records, the patient can opt for `redactionLevel: \"de-identified\"`, which forces the substrate to materialize a redacted projection rather than emit raw key-wraps.\n\n## XRPC surface (this commit)\n\n| Method | Type | Purpose |\n|---|---|---|\n| `com.etzhayyim.apps.karute.grantConsent` | procedure | Issue a new capability (server signs via `@etzhayyim/sdk`) |\n| `com.etzhayyim.apps.karute.revokeConsent` | procedure | Mark a capability revoked |\n| `com.etzhayyim.apps.karute.listConsent` | query | Enumerate capabilities by granter / grantee / purpose / status |\n| `com.etzhayyim.apps.karute.requestIryoBilling` | procedure | Bridge endpoint that verifies the capability + forwards to vendor iryo |\n\nThe lexicon record `com.etzhayyim.consent.capability` is the canonical on-chain form; the XRPC layer above is the ergonomic procedural surface.\n\n## UI integration\n\n- **Patient portal** (`60-apps/etzhayyim-project-karute/.../routes/PatientPortalView.svelte`) exposes the grant/revoke UI under the existing role=PATIENT routing. Phase 1 ships the issue + revoke + list flow with mock fallbacks.\n- **Clinician chart view** in PatientDetailView gains (Phase 2) a \"Request billing capability\" affordance that sends a notification to the patient asking for an `insurance-billing` consent for the just-closed encounter.\n\n## What this ADR does NOT change\n\n- **Substrate boundary still holds.** etzhayyim apps do not write to Kotoba/Datomic or Stripe directly — they call iryo via the bridge XRPC, which is a vendor-side actor.\n- **Charter §2 prohibitions still hold.** The capability's `purpose` enum deliberately excludes `purchase` / `subscription` / `tip` — those values remain prohibited (ADR-2605192115 + ADR-2605192130).\n- **Encryption envelope still holds.** Capabilities authorize key-wrap issuance for already-encrypted records; the records never decrypt server-side on etzhayyim's substrate.\n\n# Consequences\n\n## 正の効果\n\n- **Insurance-clinic deployment unblocks.** karute clinics that need to bill 社保 / 国保 can do so without leaving the etzhayyim substrate for their clinical operations.\n- **Patient gets a per-purpose, revocable consent surface.** Replaces the \"agree to terms\" blanket consent of legacy EHRs.\n- **One primitive, many flows.** Second-opinion, research, emergency disclosure, legal disclosure, data portability all use the same capability shape — review surface stays compact.\n- **Auditable cross-actor PHI flow.** Capability issuance + invocation + revocation are all on-chain (etzhayyim PDS or via auditor webhook).\n- **Constitutional alignment.** Vendor calls remain a progressive enhancement; the etzhayyim substrate is functional without iryo.\n\n## 負の効果 / コスト\n\n- **Revocation is observable but not cryptographically enforced.** Already-fetched ciphertext stays fetched. Mitigation: per-record rekey + tombstone (ADR-2605181100 §5.4 deferred follow-up).\n- **Capability metadata is public.** Anyone can see \"patient X granted iryo a billing capability for encounter Y on 2026-05-23\". That is the disclosure-fact leak. Mitigation: `granterDidHash` as alternative to plaintext patient DID is a deferred ADR.\n- **Two-system audit responsibility.** karute holds clinical-record provenance; iryo holds claim-record provenance. Linking them requires the capability URI as a join key.\n- **iryo MUST honor revocation in real time.** If iryo caches read-caps for performance, it must invalidate on revocation. Capability spec includes `constraints.auditWebhookDid` so the patient (or a delegated auditor) gets a notification stream and can detect honor failures.\n- **JP保険 retention** (legally-required 5-7 year retention of レセプト) overrides revocation for already-submitted claims. The capability cannot un-submit a settled claim; this limit must be stated clearly in the patient-facing UI.\n\n## Rollout\n\n1. **This commit** — Lexicons (`com.etzhayyim.consent.capability` + 4 XRPC) + ADR + actor manifest pipelines + UI integration in PatientPortalView.\n2. **Phase 2** — Real signature via `@etzhayyim/sdk.signConsentCapability` (currently a stub in the actor manifest). Server-side capability verification middleware.\n3. **Phase 3** — iryo.etzhayyim.com vendor-side `ingestKaruteEncounterForBilling` XRPC + DPC/DRG materialization pipeline. (Lives in `etzhayyim-apps-etzhayyimcojp` repo, not this one.)\n4. **Phase 4** — Auditor webhook subsystem + clinician affordance (\"Request billing consent\") in PatientDetailView. Patient-DID-hash variant for capability metadata.\n5. **Phase 5** — Per-record rekey + tombstone protocol (joint follow-up with ADR-2605181100).\n\n# Alternatives Considered\n\n## A. Direct vendor invocation without a capability primitive\n\nHave the karute actor call iryo directly on behalf of any encounter where the patient profile lists 保険証 information. Rejected: violates the explicit-consent requirement under 個保法 §17-2 (third-party provision); creates implicit blanket consent at registration time which is the legacy pattern.\n\n## B. UCAN (Ucan / Spec by ProtoSchool / Fission)\n\nUCAN is the closest comparable: capability-as-JWT, did:key principals, revocable via newer-supersedes-older. Rejected for v1 because (i) the AT Protocol substrate already gives us the record + signing key infrastructure (no need for a parallel JWT format), (ii) `did:plc` / `did:web` are the canonical principals in etzhayyim — adopting UCAN would force a `did:key` redux, (iii) UCAN's batched invocation semantics are a poor fit for clinical PHI where each access is auditable individually. The chosen shape borrows UCAN's \"purpose + caveat + delegation\" pattern but speaks AT Protocol lexicon natively.\n\n## C. ZCAP-LD\n\nZCAP-LD has the right shape (JSON-LD capability) and W3C provenance. Rejected for tooling-velocity reasons: no production AT Protocol implementations exist, and the canonicalization story (RDF dataset normalization) is heavier than CBOR. We may revisit ZCAP-LD if a wider W3C-aligned interop need emerges.\n\n## D. Centralized consent registry (vendor or etzhayyim-operated)\n\nA single registry that both sides poll for \"did patient X consent grantee Y for purpose Z\". Rejected: re-introduces the centralized-trust point ADR-2605172000 explicitly eliminates. The capability-as-PDS-record approach keeps the canonical state in the granter's own PDS where every other primitive lives.\n\n## E. Defer to OAuth scopes\n\nOAuth scopes are server-side and bound to access tokens, not records. Doesn't compose with the key-wrap mechanism (the substrate can't honor an OAuth scope without first knowing which token belongs to which session). Rejected.\n\n# References\n\n- ADR-2605231100 [karute EMR Phase 1](./2605231100-karute-emr-phase1.md)\n- ADR-2605181100 [MST encrypted records + Signal key-wrap](./2605181100-mst-encrypted-records-signal-keywrap.md)\n- ADR-2605172000 [kotoba substrate](./2605172000-etzhayyim-kotoba-substrate.md)\n- ADR-2605172400 [etzhayyim/vendor 3-axis split](./2605172400-etzhayyim-vendor-three-axis-split-rule.md)\n- ADR-2605192115 [Non-profit-only payment purpose](/90-docs/adr/2605192115-etzhayyim-non-profit-donation-only-no-ads.edn)\n- 個人情報保護法 §17, §17-2, §18, §27 (PHI 第三者提供同意)\n- 保険医療機関及び保険医療養担当規則 (レセプト記載 + 保管義務)\n- UCAN spec — https://ucan.xyz/\n- ZCAP-LD — https://w3c-ccg.github.io/zcap-spec/\n", :adr/path "90-docs/adr/2605231401-karute-consent-capability-iryo-bridge.edn", :adr/source-repo "root", :adr/topic "karute-consent-capability-iryo-bridge", :adr/last_verified "2026-05-23", :adr/date "2026-05-23", :adr/authoritative true, :adr/related ["adr-2605192115-etzhayyim-non-profit-donation-only-no-ads"], :adr/authoritative_for ["consent capability shape (`com.etzhayyim.consent.capability`)" "cross-actor PHI delegation pattern under the etzhayyim charter" "karute ↔ iryo.etzhayyim.com billing bridge" "patient-initiated grant/revoke flow in karute UI"]}]
